Trace:

  1. The only way the claimed "3-coin pool" state comes into existence is through the constructor: constructor(...,
  address _lpToken, uint8 _primaryIndex, ...). LP_TOKEN is set once, in AbstractLPOracle's constructor, from a
  deployer-supplied argument, and is immutable. There is no setter, no factory-registration path, and no permissionless
  entry point anywhere in this contract that can change or influence which pool address the oracle reads. Every read in
  _lpTokenValue() — balances(0), balances(1), get_dy(PRIMARY_INDEX, SECONDARY_INDEX, dyAmount) — targets that fixed
  immutable address.
  2. So the entire premise of the finding ("an oracle constructed over a 3-coin Curve pool") is a statement about what
  the deployer chose to pass to a contract literally named Curve2TokenOracle, whose documented domain is 2-token Curve
  pools. Pointing a two-token oracle at a three-token pool is the deployer violating the contract's stated integration
  contract, exactly like passing an unrelated ERC-20, a Chainlink feed with the wrong base, or an _primaryIndex of 7.
  Any of those "succeed silently" at construction too; that does not make them unprivileged exploits.
  3. The claimed attacker — "any unprivileged liquidator" — has no step in the claimed path that they perform. Their
  action (liquidating against a mispriced oracle) is downstream of, and entirely contingent on, the privileged
  deployment mistake having already happened. The finding never names an unprivileged trigger that causes the
  divergence; it only names someone who would profit if a trusted actor had already misconfigured the system. A
  candidate that requires "assume the deployer wired the wrong pool" is a deployment-review item, not a control-flow
  vulnerability in this code.
  4. Checking whether the code itself could drift into the 3-coin case without deployer error: SECONDARY_INDEX = 1 -
  PRIMARY_INDEX is derived at construction, TOKEN_1/TOKEN_2 are pinned from coins(0)/coins(1) at construction,
  DECIMALS_1/DECIMALS_2 likewise. All are immutable. Curve pools cannot grow coins post-deployment (N_COINS is itself
  immutable in every stableswap/stableswap-NG deployment), so a correctly-deployed 2-coin oracle cannot later become a
  3-coin oracle. There is no state transition an attacker can induce. The "bug" exists only as a static property of a
  wrong constructor argument.
  5. Secondary point, not needed for the verdict: _calculateLPTokenValue in AbstractLPOracle compares the derived spot
  price against the oracle price and enforces _lowerLimitMultiplier/_upperLimitMultiplier bounds; a systematically
  depressed valuation of the sort described would be a constant offset, not something an attacker can manipulate into or
  out of. But the dispositive fact stands on (1)–(4): the only actor in the claimed path is the deployer, acting within
  their documented permission to choose which pool this oracle serves.

  Conclusion: no unprivileged trigger, no state transition, no missing guard on any permissionless path — the claim
  reduces to "a deployer could pass an out-of-domain pool address to an immutable constructor parameter."

  CONSTRAINT|C23|A constructor-set immutable integration parameter is privileged configuration, so a candidate alleging
  harm from a wrong value must name the unprivileged action that induces or changes that value rather than assuming the
  deployer already chose an out-of-domain one.
